## 3.2 BOUNDARY STRATEGY - Four Critical Checkboxes

These four checkboxes determine what even COUNTS as a boundary pipe. Get these wrong and you'll be working with the wrong set of pipes.

### 3.2.1 "Include boundaries with unassigned areas"

**What it does:**
- **UNCHECKED (default)** : Only pipes with BOTH ends in defined zones count as boundaries
- **CHECKED**: Also counts pipes with ONE end in a zone and ONE end in unassigned area

**Visual representation:**
```
UNCHECKED:
Zone A ──── Zone B  →  ✓ Boundary
Zone A ──── Zone A  →  ✗ Not boundary (same zone)
Zone A ──── Outside →  ✗ Not boundary (ignored)

CHECKED:
Zone A ──── Zone B  →  ✓ Boundary
Zone A ──── Zone A  →  ✗ Not boundary (same zone)
Zone A ──── Outside →  ✓ Now a boundary
Zone B ──── Outside →  ✓ Now a boundary
```

**When to check:**
- You want complete hydraulic isolation analysis
- Your network has significant peripheral areas
- You're studying how zones connect to the rest of the system

**When to uncheck:**
- You only care about inter-zone connections
- Outside areas are irrelevant to your study
- You want to simplify and focus on core zone interactions

**Real-world example:** A city with defined pressure zones but also outlying areas. Checked = including pipes to the outskirts. Unchecked = only pipes between downtown zones.

### 3.2.2 "Show zone boundaries on map"

**What it does:**
- Toggles the colored zone polygons ON/OFF
- Does NOT affect boundary identification, only visualization

**When to check (show polygons):**
- You're learning the zone layout
- You need to see which zone is which
- You're presenting to someone unfamiliar with the zones

**When to uncheck (hide polygons):**
- You're focused on pipe details
- Polygons are obscuring small pipes
- You're using animation (animations look cleaner without polygons)
- You're using colormap (node colors show better without polygons underneath)

### 3.2.3 "Hide closed boundaries"

**What it does:**
- **UNCHECKED**: Shows ALL boundaries (green=open, red=closed)
- **CHECKED**: Shows ONLY open boundaries (green), hides all red pipes completely

**Visual comparison:**
```
UNCHECKED (Show everything):     CHECKED (Hide closed):
[Green]──Open    Visible         [Green]──Open    Visible
[Red]────Closed  Visible         [Red]────Closed  INVISIBLE
[Green]──Open    Visible         [Green]──Open    Visible
[Red]────Closed  Visible         [Red]────Closed  INVISIBLE
```

**When to uncheck (show all):**
- You need to see where closed boundaries ARE
- You're reviewing your work
- You want the complete picture

**When to check (hide closed):**
- You only care about what's STILL open
- You want to de-clutter the view
- You're checking if any connections remain between zones
- You're using Interactive Mode and only want to see available open pipes

**Power user tip:** Use this with Interactive Mode. Hide closed boundaries, then click the remaining green pipes to close them one by one. As you close each pipe, it disappears from view - giving you instant visual feedback that you've eliminated that connection.

### 3.2.4 "Reset before applying"

**What it does:**
- **CHECKED**: Before applying new automatic selections, CLOSE ALL boundaries first, THEN open the newly selected ones
- **UNCHECKED**: Keep current open boundaries, ADD newly selected ones to them

**When to check (reset first):**
- You want a clean slate
- You're experimenting with different strategies
- You want to ensure results are ONLY from current settings

**When to uncheck (preserve existing):**
- You've manually opened some pipes you want to keep
- You're building up connections incrementally
- You want to ADD to current configuration, not replace it

**Example:** You manually opened 3 pipes. Then you set automatic to keep 2 per pair.
- If CHECKED: Your 3 manual opens get CLOSED, only automatic selections remain
- If UNCHECKED: Your 3 manual opens stay OPEN, automatic selections ADD to them

