# PART 11: ANIMATION DEEP DIVE

## 11.1 How Animation Works

When you click ▶️ Animate:

```
Step 1: Collect all OPEN boundaries (green pipes)

Step 2: For each open boundary:
        - Get its start point (x1,y1)
        - Get its end point (x2,y2)
        - Calculate flow direction from simulation
        - Create a moving dot

Step 3: Position dots initially:
        - Random positions along each pipe
        - (Not all starting at same place)

Step 4: Start timer (updates every ~100ms)

Step 5: Each timer tick:
        - Move each dot along its pipe
        - Distance moved = speed × direction
        - If dot reaches end, wrap to start
        - Redraw all dots at new positions

Step 6: Continue until stopped
```

## 11.2 What Animation Shows

**Flow direction:**
- Dots move from start node to end node = positive flow
- Dots move from end node to start node = negative flow
- This matches flow direction from simulation

**Flow presence:**
- Moving dots = flow > 0
- Stationary or no dots = zero flow
- Speed doesn't indicate flow magnitude (just visualization)

**Connectivity:**
- Which boundaries are actually carrying water
- Paths water takes between zones

## 11.3 Animation Behavior Details

**Dot appearance:**
- Gold color (#FFD700)
- Small circles with black outline
- Visible against all backgrounds

**Movement:**
- Smooth continuous motion
- Wraps around (reaches end, reappears at start)
- Multiple dots per pipe (if multiple positions active)

**Auto-stop events:**
- Changing any boundary (click or automatic)
- Closing the application
- Clicking Animate button again

**Performance:**
- Optimized for thousands of pipes
- Uses vectorized operations (no per-pipe loops)
- Should be smooth even on large networks

## 11.4 Using Animation Effectively

### 11.4.1 Before Animating

1. **Run simulation** (⚡) - animation needs flow data
2. **Set your boundaries** - only open pipes animate
3. **Hide zone polygons** - cleaner view (happens automatically)
4. **Consider hiding closed boundaries** - focus on active pipes

### 11.4.2 While Animating

- Watch overall flow patterns
- Note which boundaries carry flow
- See if flow direction matches expectations
- Identify zero-flow boundaries (no dots)

### 11.4.3 After Animating

- Click again to stop
- Zone polygons reappear
- Can make changes and restart

## 11.5 Animation Limitations

- Only shows open boundaries (green)
- Speed is fixed (not adjustable)
- No indication of flow magnitude (just presence/direction)
- Stops automatically on any change

